Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.comp.admin.policy,comp.org.eff.talk
From: rick@trystro.uucp (Richard E. Nickle)
Subject:  Re: Student suspended :: User Guidelines
Message-ID: <C7n5LK.721@trystro.uucp>
Date: Wed, 26 May 1993 15:56:07 GMT

In article <1tto15$nub@menudo.uh.edu> st10t@Rosie.UH.EDU writes:
>===============================================================================
>
>                        Security Guidelines for Users
>            Adapted from Computing Facilities User Guidelines (1/91)
>
>
>   The University of Houston Information Technology Division exists to
>serve faculty, staff and students of the University in support of instructional
>and research activities.  University computing facilities are a public resource
>and may not be used for personal or corporate profit.  The following conditions
>apply to all users of the computing facilities:
>
> [ relatively reasonable rules deleted to save bandwidth ]
>	(5) The user shall not engage in deliberately wasteful practices such
>	as printing large amounts of unnecessary output, performing unnecessary
>	computations, simultaneously queuing multiple batch jobs and holding 
>	unused tape drives and telephone lines.>
Performing unnecessary computations?  That's pretty all-inclusive.  This implies
that I could be expelled from school for particularly bad programming practices.

main(int argc, char **argv)
{
	int	c = 0;  	/* does this unnecessary assignment of zero, 
				   followed by a second assignment in the loop
				   code constitute 'unnecessary computation'?
				   or is my ass saved by a good optimizer?  */

	for (c = 0; c < 100; c++) {
		printf("%d\n",c);
	}
}

Just to clue you in, but the whole process of programming involves quite
a *BIT* of 'unnecessary computation'.  It's difficult to learn anything
about the art of programming without squandering a lot of CPU cycles
running tests of algorithms, sample programs, and making a lot of errors
(yes, even the 'error' of attempting to find out more about the system
by poking around in the different directories finding out what all the
little parts do).  

I say your student, by looking at the /etc/passwd file was at worst
attempting to gain access to information that would make no threat
to your site's security, at best accessing information which he
may have needed for some legitimate purpose, and at *least* engaging
in a human characteristic which is fundamental to the learning
process:  curiosity.  If I was interviewing a student from your
school for a Unix related job who *didn't* know what the /etc/passwd
file did, where it was, and what it's characteristics were, I 
certainly wouldn't hire him.

>	(6) The user shall not engage in behavior that creates an unpleasant
>	environment for other users.
And the University's responsibilty is to create an environment conducive
to learning.  You also have responsibilities.  It seems to me, barring
some evidence of misconduct that the University has not presented, that
the charges are based on ignorance on the part of the school, not facts. 

I understand that the University is more at risk for litigation than the
student, and that you may be witholding some piece of information, but
unless this kid is an axe-murderer or something, I'd reinstate him and
let this whole thing drop right now.  It's definitely hurting your image.

